{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2146 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "195d811f004aebb624e814bfbcd2621e32342c418d9d9b2afa0ca1e1c7611f7c" :edb/db-before-t 2145 :edb/db-after-t 2146 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535458 50 #inst "2026-10-09T00:31:29.476Z" 13194139535458 true] [17592186171780 1100 "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139535458 true] [17592186171780 1100 "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $3 \\textrm{rad/s}$. Given that the object is at the position $(7,0)$ when $t=0$, find the position function of the particle." 13194139535458 false] [17592186171780 1145 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O$, and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0$.\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0)$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i\n$$\n\nand we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7$.\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and\n\n$$\n\\omega= -3 \\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\n(negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= 7(\\cos (-3t)i + \\sin (-3t)j) \\\\\n&= 7(\\cos 3ti - \\sin 3tj)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 false] [17592186171780 1145 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos \\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin \\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(7,0),$ we have that $\\mathbf r(0)=\\,7\\mathbf i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=7.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=7$ and $\\omega= -3  \\textrm{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3t)\\,j) \\\\  & =7(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 true] [17592186171781 1100 "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." 13194139535458 false] [17592186171781 1100 "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 5\\cos \\pi t \\, \\mathbf i + 5\\sin \\pi t \\,\\mathbf j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139535458 true] [17592186171781 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= (r(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (5\\cos πti + 5\\sin πtj)^{′} \\\\\n&=-5π\\sin πti + 5π\\cos πtj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid v(t) \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-5π\\sin πt)^{2} + (5π\\cos πt)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25π^{2}\\sin^{2} πt + 25π^{2}\\cos^{2} πt} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25π^{2}(\\sin^{2} πt + \\cos^{2} πt)} \\\\\n&= 5π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{m/s}$.\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid = R \\mid \\omega \\mid\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nR=5\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand the angular frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf v \\mid \\mid = 5\\cdot \\mid \\pi \\mid = 5\\pi \\textrm{m/s}\n$$" 13194139535458 false] [17592186171781 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-5\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)^{2}+(5\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+25{\\pi }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\pi }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\pi t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\pi t)} \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the object's speed is $5 \\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = R|\\omega|,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega$ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=5\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = \\pi \\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v|| = 5\\cdot |\\pi| = 5\\pi \\textrm{ m/s}.\n$$" 13194139535458 true] [17592186171782 1100 "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\,  \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139535458 true] [17592186171782 1100 "An object moves along a circular path with position $\\mathbf {r}(t)= 4(\\cos 3t\\, \\mathbf i + \\sin 3t\\, \\mathbf j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." 13194139535458 false] [17592186171782 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= (r(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (4(\\cos 3ti + \\sin 3tj))^{′} \\\\\n&=-12\\sin 3ti + 12\\cos 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= (v(t))^{′} \\\\\n&= (-12\\sin 3ti + 12\\cos 3tj)^{′} \\\\\n&=-36\\cos 3ti - 36\\sin 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mid a(t) \\mid \\mid &= \\sqrt{(-36\\cos 3t)^{2} + (-36\\sin 3t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2}\\cos^{2} 3t + 36^{2}\\sin^{2} 3t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36^{2}} \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nNote that $\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad \\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid = \\omega^2 \\mid \\mid \\mathbf r \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\na=4\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nand the angular frequency\n\n$$\n\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf a \\mid \\mid = 3^2\\cdot \\mid 4 \\mid = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2\n$$" 13194139535458 false] [17592186171782 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-36\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-36\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{36}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{36}^{2}} \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nNote that $||\\mathbf a||$ is constant, but $\\mathbf a$ is not.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2\\mathbf r \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad ||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2||\\mathbf r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=4\\,\\textrm m$ and the angular frequency $\\omega = 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = 3^2\\cdot |4| = 36\\, \\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$" 13194139535458 true] [17592186171974 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350251 13194139535458 true] [17592186171974 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350258 13194139535458 true] [17592186171974 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350265 13194139535458 true] [17592186171974 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350272 13194139535458 true] [17592186171974 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350279 13194139535458 true] [17592186171975 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350286 13194139535458 true] [17592186171975 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350293 13194139535458 true] [17592186171975 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350300 13194139535458 true] [17592186171975 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350307 13194139535458 true] [17592186171976 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350314 13194139535458 true] [17592186171976 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350321 13194139535458 true] [17592186171976 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350328 13194139535458 true] [17592186171976 1020 #edb/ref 17592186350335 13194139535458 true] [17592186172577 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535458 true] [17592186172577 1100 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t) = 2\\cos 3ti + 2\\sin 3tj$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t$, measured in seconds." 13194139535458 false] [17592186172577 1100 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139535458 true] [17592186172577 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡3t+36{cos}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡3t+{cos}^{2}⁡3t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=2\\cdot |3|=6\\text{ m/s}.\n$$" 13194139535458 true] [17592186172580 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535458 true] [17592186172580 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Given that the particle is located at $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the acceleration of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ seconds." 13194139535458 true] [17592186172580 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along a counterclockwise circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2rad/s$. Given that the particle is located at $(3, 0)$ when $t = 0$, find the acceleration of the particle at $t = \\frac{π}{6}$ seconds." 13194139535458 false] [17592186172580 1145 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3$ and $\\omega =2\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,j \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,i-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{2}\\,i-\\frac{12\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =-6\\,i-6\\sqrt{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350251 1017 #edb/ref 17592186350252 13194139535458 true] [17592186350251 1089 "q-97631" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350251 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535458 true] [17592186350251 1100 "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $1\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139535458 true] [17592186350251 1113 #uuid "2ad5331c-5ea7-58c3-ac36-0eb50db95bf5" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350251 1145 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(4,0),$ we have that $r(0)=4i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=4.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=4$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350253 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350254 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350255 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350256 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350257 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1041 "selection" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1104 #edb/ref 17592186350254 13194139535458 true] [17592186350252 1110 #uuid "cdc807c5-84cd-5557-ad56-9431f3b3b90f" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350253 1034 "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350253 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535458 true] [17592186350253 1111 #uuid "cadb88ee-225b-5f6a-9b83-faa4f40265f5" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350254 1034 "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350254 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139