314 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535458 true] [17592186350314 1100 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139535458 true] [17592186350314 1113 #uuid "93547497-1bc4-5a24-a92c-ea680655d623" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350314 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}+(-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{16}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡4t+{16}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{16}^{2}} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=1\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={4}^{2}\\cdot 1=16\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535458 true] [17592186350315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350316 13194139535458 true] [17592186350315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350317 13194139535458 true] [17592186350315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350318 13194139535458 true] [17592186350315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186350319 